Z||000016||StuGood||03-29-2001||03:36 PM||||Remember, there were different steering rack ratios available in Fieros.  So maybe these "one-handed" guys (and gals) have a secret (mechanical) advantage you don't.  Tires and inflation make a difference, too, of course.<P>Just trying to make you feel good, mostly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||03-29-2001||03:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hank is Here:<BR><B>Well I would have to answer yes and no <P>My GT I need two hands<BR>My 88 coupe one hand no problem<P>I attirbutre it to the tire width and also the steering ratio</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The '88's smaller scrub radius makes steering effort lighter with same rack ratio and tires.<P>I have 235/60-15's on the front of my '87 GT (3.0 turn rack) and I can one hand it easily enough. The taller tires reduce the scrub radius, and thus the steering effort, significantly.<P>Being 6'1" and weighing ~250lbs doesn't hurt either.<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.cyberpump.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberpump.com</A>  if you think you're not strong enough to handle your Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||stsmithgt||03-29-2001||06:50 PM||||I went from a 66 Chevy truck with no, nothing, and nada for power so the GT was no problem.  I even put a smaller diameter wheel on to get more leg room.  Your power steering NOW is made by "Armstrong". <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  The difficulty will also depend on how much tread you have left on the tire.  New tires are more forgiving than slicks.  I suppose the new tires have more flex in the tread and slicks have a larger contact patch stuck on the ground. ? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">?||||reg||3
Z||000019||DavidHenshaw||03-30-2001||02:18 AM||||Oh this is so embarassing. I have to use two hands. Guess I'm not as macho as I thought. Could this be why my wife calls me Mr. Wimp?<P>I don't care! I love this car and I don't care who knows it! Impractical, yes. Small yes. Oh! But when you sit behind that wheel and you feel like you're wearing the car.<P>Dave<P>86 GT ||||reg||1
Z||000020||frontal lobe||03-30-2001||02:24 AM||||I was afraid I was going to get the female members responding that they had no problems one-handing the wheel!  Guess it's time for creatine shakes for breakfast and powerbars sticking out of the ashtrays, ready for consumption at all times.  Then 20 times around the block each way one-handing the wheel to bulk up.||||reg||1
Z||000021||StuGood||03-30-2001||11:42 AM||||Just be sure to change hands periodically.  You don't want to end up with your left arm looking like Popeye's, and your right arm like Olive Oyl's -! <P>Maybe you're a good candidate for the "auto-power-down" power windows project?  That way, you blip the "down" button for an instant, and the window rolls all the way down - leaving both hands free to steer...<P>I believe the circuit / plans appeared in the Web Electric magazine sometime within the past year.  Could be a fun "upgrade" project for your car - especially as someone <B>else</B> has already worked out the details for you.  Personally, I love to learn from others' experiences   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !<BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 03-30-2001).]||||reg||12
